00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.860
So why is live
video so difficult?

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.970
How many people
here have watched

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.970
any type of video stream,
and it's been buffering,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:53.730
and you've waited on
either a phone, a browser--

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:54.230
yeah.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.570
I would say everyone, right?

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:57.590
So why is--

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:58.880
GEIR MAGNUSSON: And for those
that haven't, have you just not

00:01:58.880 --> 00:01:59.960
looked at video on the internet?

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.085
NEEVE NIKOO: [LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:02:01.085 --> 00:02:04.640
So this is a really hard
problem to solve, actually,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.660
and there's a lot of
things to consider.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.450
So just real quick, to level
set and get a good picture

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:12.750
of what's going on--

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.670
so with live video
distribution, there's

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.238
these different
components that you have.

00:02:19.238 --> 00:02:20.780
So first you have
to actually acquire

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.580
all of your different signals.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.190
So this can be via
satellite, camera, fiber.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.500
There's many different
ways to do so.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.980
Then you're going to take that
into an MPEG transport stream,

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.320
and you're going to do
a bunch of processing--

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.340
encoding, transcoding, having
your digital rights management,

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.730
logos that are needed,
and get the video stream

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.460
ready to actually deliver.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.770
Then, now, how do I actually
deliver this globally?

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.410
So I need to partner
with many CDN providers

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.040
to then get and distribute
this to all the different types

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.150
of devices.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.610
So this is a really,
really, really, really--

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.740
I'll just keep saying
that-- high level

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.920
of the end-to-end video
distribution workflow.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.050
We'll go into more detail
and technical depth,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.020
but I just wanted
to set the stage.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.620
So what are all the
different things

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.700
that we have to consider?

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.570
So this has been an
industry that's been around

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.330
for quite a number of years--

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.410
broadcast.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.030
This has been done
via satellite,

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.340
where I have a set, fixed
amount of bandwidth,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.700
and it's just been new protocols
that have come out every 5,

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.300
10 years or so that's
basically compressed the data.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.560
So how much data can I fit
with a fixed bandwidth rate?

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.670
Now there's many
different devices.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:37.820
So that brings a bunch
of different problems.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.990
And when I'm sending
video over the internet,

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.140
I have to account for all
the different devices,

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.040
the different video formats that
are needed based on the device

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.740
that I have for the
picture quality.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:50.850
And in that workflow,
in order to do that,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.370
there's so many
different partners

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.710
that you're going to have
to actually go through with.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:58.160
So in Fubo's case,
they have to acquire

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.140
all these different
types of content

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.910
from many different providers.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:03.230
So there's CBS.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:03.980
There's Fox.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.500
There's all the
different sports streams.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.750
And that's just on
the content part,

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.300
not even including who
they choose to partner

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:15.320
with on the video processing
side, the CDN side, et cetera.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.890
So there's many different parts
and many different companies

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.779
that are in the
entire workflow that

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:23.900
can really make this a pretty
tough problem to actually

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:24.860
solve.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.440
And then not to say how
much bandwidth there is--

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.650
so there's different bandwidth
at each part in that workflow.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:34.340
And then, at the end device, I
can have really poor internet

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.670
because I'm a cheap guy.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.400
I only have 20
megabits per second.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.650
How am I going to get
high-quality video compared

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:42.950
to somebody who's paying
for the one gigabit line?

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.330
So there's many, many
different problems that we see.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.600
And then the coverage
area globally--

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.910
so there's going to be
people where there's maybe

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.460
not infrastructure that
is really best served

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:53.160
in that area.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.390
So how can I serve
someone in the middle

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:59.365
of nowhere versus a
large metropolitan city?

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:00.740
So these are all
different things

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:06.470
that we have to consider
when distributing live video.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.910
So where can Google help?

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.300
So we have the
largest cloud network.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.160
I had to show the
beautiful scaled slide

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.050
that you've probably seen in 15
other different presentations.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.550
But in all seriousness, we
have an extremely impressive

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.280
network, over 100
points of presence,

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.210
and we really think this can
help with scaling large video.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.430
We have many different
applications that I'm sure

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.090
we all use, and so we really
think we help with scale,

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.660
especially with live
video distribution.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:38.080
So there's a couple
different solution pathways

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.430
that you can go when trying to
solve the problem of live video

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:43.420
distribution.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.690
So the spectrum is
infrastructure as a service--

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.210
you see it on the
left-hand side--

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.650
all the way to
software as a service.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:56.740
So we've seen multiple clients
and partners of ours do both.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:00.490
It just depends where you want
to turn the different knobs.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:02.890
So on the right-hand
side, we have

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:11.080
products we call Anvato, which
is our video processing suite.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.312
We've seen NBC and
CBS use this before.

00:06:14.312 --> 00:06:16.270
And then on the left, we
see different partners

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.810
such as Fubo who use our
infrastructure as a service

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.200
solution set, so using
different things like GKE,

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:28.660
Compute Engine, and other GCP
offerings that we've seen.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:29.830
So it just depends.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:33.970
Infrastructure as a service
gives you the most flexibility

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:37.690
in your deployment, and you can
really control different parts

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:39.470
in the video
distribution workflow,

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:41.800
whereas software as a service
gives you what you need,

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.530
but you have a little
bit less control on that.

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:47.080
And we see this with a
bunch of Cloud offerings

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:51.010
today, with serverless and
then also Compute Engine.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.810
So there's just
different methodologies

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.790
that you can do so,
and I think that's what

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.200
makes Cloud a really
unique and fun place to be.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.870
We give you the
tool sets, and you

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.107
can choose what makes
the most sense for you

00:07:01.107 --> 00:07:02.690
in your business and
your application.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.900
So I've kind of set
the stage talking

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.720
about what the problem
is, different things

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.300
that you have to consider
throughout the video

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.450
distribution workflow, and
the different offerings we

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:16.210
think that GCP can help with.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:18.370
Now I'm going to
hand it over to Geir

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:23.920
to talk about Fubo, their
journey on GCP, and the reason

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.775
that they chose the
specific architecture

00:07:25.775 --> 00:07:27.400
and different components
that they did.

