00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:19.300
So Fubo is a OTT
streaming service.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:20.760
We are sports-focused.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:24.510
We call ourselves the
world's only sports-focused.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:27.180
It really comes from our
origin as a provider that

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.410
was delivering for soccer, and I
think the name came from a riff

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.289
on football.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:39.637
What we do is we deliver both
live television and VOD to you.

00:08:39.637 --> 00:08:41.220
And when we say live
television, these

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.970
are the channels that you would
get from any other provider--

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:51.310
Fox, Turner, et cetera, sports
stations like BN Sports.

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:54.240
You'll get NBCSN, NBC stations.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.870
We have locals, so just your
local television affiliates

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.780
for Fox, NBC.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.520
We also have regional
sports nets, so--

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.190
I don't know--

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:10.730
NESN in Boston or SNY in New
York for your local sports.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:13.023
And it's available on
pretty much anything

00:09:13.023 --> 00:09:14.440
that you would
want to show it on.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.898
We are trying to
make it a really

00:09:16.898 --> 00:09:18.690
great in-home experience,
so you can get it

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:22.440
to your smart TV or your
various TV-connected devices

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.412
like an Apple TV or Fire TV.

00:09:24.412 --> 00:09:25.870
But you can also
get it on the web.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.452
You can also get it
on mobile devices,

00:09:28.452 --> 00:09:29.910
pretty much anywhere
you want to be

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.323
as long as there's bandwidth.

00:09:34.723 --> 00:09:36.140
So a little bit
of our backstory--

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.870
we'll get into some
technical things in a moment.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:43.340
The original product vision
was to aggregate VODs

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.510
for professional football clubs,
or teams, as they're called.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.760
NEEVE NIKOO: That's soccer
for anyone who is American.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:50.718
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Soccer.

00:09:50.718 --> 00:09:52.790
Yeah, sorry.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.951
It's this foreign sport
that's really popular.

00:09:54.951 --> 00:09:55.618
NEEVE NIKOO: Ha.

00:09:58.325 --> 00:10:00.200
GEIR MAGNUSSON: What
the clubs were doing is,

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.305
on their website, they
would have content specific

00:10:02.305 --> 00:10:02.930
to their teams.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:06.890
So you go to Everton and you can
see game replays, highlights,

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:10.820
interviews, backstory
kind of stuff.

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:14.870
But a lot of soccer fans
like multiple teams.

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:17.810
So what Fubo was trying to
do was bring it all together

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:22.760
in one place where you can get
this content for all of soccer.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:25.100
And it didn't quite
work because--

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:26.870
at least in a market sense--

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:30.000
sports fans like live sports.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:30.500
Right?

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.008
Anybody here who
is a sports fan?

00:10:33.008 --> 00:10:34.550
Do you tend to want
to watch it live,

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:37.080
or are you willing to record
it and watch it next week?

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.280
Live, right?

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.140
So Fubo had to bring
in live streaming.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.350
And while VOD is fairly
straightforward--

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:48.450
because you can get the
asset ahead of time,

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.840
you can transcode it,
prepare it, quality control

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.120
and make sure it's a good
experience for the consumer,

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.940
and then put it out in
your distribution system--

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:59.290
live is a real-time thing.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:10:59.790
Right?

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.600
It's being broadcast.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.202
And when I say live, I'll also
use linear interchangeably.

00:11:05.202 --> 00:11:06.660
For me, live is
actually true live,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.660
when people are out on
the field doing something

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:12.510
or like an awards show or
something where it's actually

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:14.460
happening in real time.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:16.110
Linear is just the
general idea that we

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.240
have these streams
being broadcast at us,

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.410
and they need to be delivered as
close to real time as possible.

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:22.660
And it cannot be in real time.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:24.380
There's latency.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:28.730
It's just physics and
architecture forces.

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:32.790
But adding this linear stream
became a really big change

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:35.550
for the business,
both technically

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.525
and as well from the
perspective of the business.

00:11:38.525 --> 00:11:40.900
And I'll give a little bit of
background on the business.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.060
We're a cable company.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.220
We're no different in
some ways than whoever

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:45.690
your cable provider is.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:49.080
We have to license the content
from the content owners--

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:52.035
Fox, Hallmark, whoever.

00:11:52.035 --> 00:11:53.670
And we use that
same business model

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:55.740
that the cable
companies do, which

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:59.490
is, we have to pay on a per
subscriber basis, not what's

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:00.820
viewed.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:03.870
But if we have a quarter
million subscribers,

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:08.160
we will pay 250,000 times
whatever the per subscriber

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.210
fee is.

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:12.930
And this makes a pretty tough
business because a lot of money

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:17.250
goes into purchasing the content
or licensing the content.

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:21.720
In fact, in our business,
we don't quite even

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.460
break even on subscriber fees.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:26.130
Subscriber fees plus
a little bit more

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:28.020
are given all to
the content owners.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:29.850
And our business is
about making it up

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.900
from advertising and
other content upsells.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.590
On the technology side--

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:38.670
and this is where we're
going to go into in a moment.

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:42.960
Adding linear, it was a
really big difference.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.210
The plane is always in the air.

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:51.290
You can never stop.

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:54.260
You can never pause.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:55.550
It just has to work.

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:57.750
And we're competing
with the system that

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:02.480
has over 70 years of development
behind it and is fairly stable.

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:05.540
When you turn on your television
on a legacy distribution

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:07.780
system, it works.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:09.440
And it works really well.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:10.940
And for us to be
successful, we have

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:14.360
to deliver that kind
of consumer experience

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.430
without owning the
infrastructure,

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:19.920
without owning the
network path to the user,

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.292
and it's a bit of a trick.

00:13:22.292 --> 00:13:23.750
Apparently my slide
changed itself.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.170
So why is this hard?

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:35.950
I think it's because
we're all figuring it out.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.750
Certainly the content
owners, our partners,

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:38.630
are figuring it out.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:41.950
We are a different kind of place
for content to be pushed to.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:43.750
We have different requirements.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:45.460
We're in different places.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:49.207
We don't have satellite
downlink infrastructure.

00:13:49.207 --> 00:13:50.540
We don't have broadcast centers.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.807
We are in the cloud.

00:13:53.807 --> 00:13:55.390
Single acquisition
partners are people

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.540
who specialize in transport,
of getting it from the content

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:00.790
partner to the cloud.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:06.420
They are also figuring it
out because, again, it's

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:07.900
a new thing to
get into the cloud

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.940
and to integrate with
these other parties.

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:12.810
Our Cloud partners
are figuring it out.

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:14.850
We do a kind of
workload that isn't very

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:16.668
common in cloud environments.

00:14:16.668 --> 00:14:18.210
Some people think
it's a little crazy

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.270
to do in a cloud environment
with the variability

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:23.380
in multitenancy that
you're going to run into,

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.060
but we make it work.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:27.390
CDN partners-- same thing.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:28.770
We're certainly figuring it out.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:31.960
And then consumers also
are part of the chain.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.530
They are effectively part
of the distribution system

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:39.470
in that their in-home networks
are part of the delivery path.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:41.880
The different kinds
of devices that they

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.100
would need to be able to
have a good OTT experience

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:45.350
is also something new to them.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.990
We'll get to technical
stuff in a moment.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:54.900
Our timeline is, we were
founded in 2015, entirely

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:56.940
soccer-focused.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.490
We talked about how we went
to VOD, adding a linear,

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:02.550
but that really wasn't
a success in the market

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:07.445
either because any sport
is limited in terms of time

00:15:07.445 --> 00:15:08.820
and limited in
terms of interest.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:09.960
You have your season--

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.090
baseball season, I
guess, which seems

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:14.940
to go on forever, like hockey.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.640
But football season's in
the fall, early winter.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.390
Soccer season is limited.

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:21.510
So what we needed
to do is to make it

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:23.850
something that you would
subscribe to and keep all year

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:24.570
round.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.017
We had to have other content.

00:15:27.017 --> 00:15:28.600
NEEVE NIKOO: The
slide keeps changing.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:30.130
I don't know why.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:31.560
But it--

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:33.510
GEIR MAGNUSSON: If I
just keep jumping--

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:34.688
NEEVE NIKOO: Ha.

00:15:34.688 --> 00:15:36.230
GEIR MAGNUSSON: So
we added Fox, NBC.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.940
We added CBS-- so now,
starting to produce

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:42.810
a content package that's
really for the whole family.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:46.380
We launched advanced DVR, which
is a table stakes if you're

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:49.740
going to have an OTT service,
hit 100,000 subscribers

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:54.480
by the end of 2017,
really focused now

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:57.180
on building a business out of
it by adding server-side ad

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:00.510
insertion, which is
the main monetization

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.530
driver for the business.

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:04.860
What we do is, on every
stream that's coming

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:07.800
to every single user, they're
all individual streams,

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.470
and we're able to insert
advertising on a per user,

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:11.730
per stream basis.

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:14.092
We'll talk about that
a little bit later.

00:16:14.092 --> 00:16:16.300
Apparently, it's a big event
that I joined the place,

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:18.510
but I've been there
for about a year.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:22.140
We were the first to
do 4K streaming OTT,

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.060
and we did it with the World
Cup, which was last year.

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:30.090
That's a large event played
mostly by foreigners-- soccer.

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:32.120
How many people are soccer fans?

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:32.670
OK.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:33.900
So you knew what
the World Cup was.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:35.070
NEEVE NIKOO:
Football and soccer--

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:36.403
we're going to interchange that.

00:16:36.403 --> 00:16:40.290
Just-- we're not
confusing anyone, so--

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:42.420
GEIR MAGNUSSON: We have
an international strategy.

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:44.850
We launched into
Spain, and then we

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:47.910
keep adding new
content to the package.

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:53.130
So how many people know
how streaming really works?

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:57.630
It's not a continuous stream
of bits being sent at you.

00:16:57.630 --> 00:16:59.370
It is really a
series of files that

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:02.430
are just being loaded over,
and buffered, and then played

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:03.390
at you.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:06.185
So I'm going to give
this as a background,

00:17:06.185 --> 00:17:08.310
talking about something
called HTTP live streaming.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.697
It's a fairly simple
protocol, some

00:17:10.697 --> 00:17:12.780
would argue one that should
never have made it out

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:15.390
of the design room.

00:17:18.119 --> 00:17:23.030
But the way it works
is, you have a source.

00:17:23.030 --> 00:17:26.000
Usually it's a pretty fat
source, 30 megabits a second,

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:29.320
50 megabits a second,
depending on the quality of it.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:30.530
It is encoded.

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:33.560
And what that means is we take
that sort of mezzanine feed

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:36.230
and we turn it into
multiple bit rates.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:42.670
So there may be a 10 megabit
stream, a 7, a 5, a 3, 1, 750K,

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:43.790
whatever you choose to do.

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:45.710
It's the variant
ladder, it's called.

00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:49.100
And the reason why you do that
is, it allows the player to,

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:53.420
based on the trouble it's
having loading content,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:56.690
switch to different bit rates to
keep continuity of the stream.

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:59.030
You're going to lose quality
as the bit rate goes down,

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:03.540
but you at least
get the experience.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.690
Now, what encoding
is doing, it's

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:07.910
creating little files
that are called manifests.

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:12.500
It's a text file that is
a list of the next series

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:15.980
of video segments that are
to be played by the player.

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:18.260
And then video segments
are just chunks of video

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:22.310
that are being produced and
then dropped into storage.

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:27.110
So these would be, say,
S3 buckets somewhere.

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:29.032
Or I guess I misspelled--

00:18:29.032 --> 00:18:30.065
NEEVE NIKOO: You spelled
that wrong, actually.

00:18:30.065 --> 00:18:30.950
GEIR MAGNUSSON: I
misspelled that.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:31.010
Right.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:31.130
It's actually--

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:32.070
NEEVE NIKOO: It's spelled GCS.

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:32.210
So--

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:32.630
GEIR MAGNUSSON: GCS.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:34.047
NEEVE NIKOO: But
that's all right.

00:18:34.047 --> 00:18:34.970
That's fine.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:35.900
GEIR MAGNUSSON: So what
the player is doing

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:37.733
is the player's going
to ask for a manifest.

00:18:37.733 --> 00:18:40.310
It'll say, give me the next
manifest, takes the manifest,

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:42.102
and then starts loading
the video segments.

00:18:42.102 --> 00:18:44.600
And when it has enough of
them, it'll start the playback.

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:46.100
But then it keeps
this process going

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.740
while the video's playing,
asking for the next manifest.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:52.010
Every four seconds, it
asks for a manifest.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:53.810
And these manifests
keep getting updated

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:56.330
as new chunks are being
created off the encoder,

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:57.890
and playback occurs.

00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:00.560
So this is fundamentally
how HLS works.

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:01.750
And DASH is very similar.

00:19:01.750 --> 00:19:04.890
It's a different standard,
but it's basically--

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:07.640
give me a list of things
to do, start playing it,

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:11.030
keep fetching, keep giving
me the list, and so on.

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:13.385
NEEVE NIKOO: Apple thanks
you for your explanation.

00:19:13.385 --> 00:19:15.260
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Yeah,
so it's a call out to--

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:16.910
I feel like I should be selling
something here on the floor,

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:17.132
anyway.

00:19:17.132 --> 00:19:17.799
NEEVE NIKOO: Ha.

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:23.390
GEIR MAGNUSSON: So the
obligatory "how this maps

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:26.150
into Google services" slide--

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:29.150
ingestion is on the
networking infrastructure.

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:31.590
Google has an excellent
networking infrastructure.

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:35.630
So we bring a lot of streams
right into us that way.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:37.940
We do the encoding
or the transcoding

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:39.640
using Google Compute.

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:41.450
We are dropping into
multiple storages

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:43.620
for redundancy,
multiple regions.

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:48.260
And then a delivery
network such as Google CDN

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:53.210
is used to front the
origins, and the devices

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:56.600
pull in the way we just
described with the HLS lesson.

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:02.000
Now, what we do is a
little more complicated

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:05.480
than the previous slide.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:07.230
There's a couple
of pieces of this.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:10.010
We've got third-party inputs.

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:13.170
That would be-- content
is the biggest one.

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:15.710
And there's some content
providers up there.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:19.080
We get Metadata because,
as part of any television,

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:21.770
you want to have a guide, like
an electronic program guide.

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:23.950
So we need to get
that information,

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.720
and that comes from a
company called Gracenote.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:30.080
Tribune Media Services
is the subsidiary.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.540
There's other kinds of data
sources that can come in.

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.082
And then there's
third-party services

00:20:34.082 --> 00:20:37.975
that we use for mainly noncore
things, such as subscription

00:20:37.975 --> 00:20:38.975
management and payments.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:44.040
More on that in a sec.

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:47.250
Then inside this big gray
box is really what we do.

00:20:49.343 --> 00:20:50.760
If you can think
of-- the platform

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:52.093
divides into a couple of pieces.

00:20:52.093 --> 00:20:54.030
There's video
management and delivery.

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:57.600
There is program, data,
and discovery, and then--

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:01.140
program, comma, data,
and discovery-- and then

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:04.210
user and subscription management
are the three big chunks.

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:06.480
And then there's, finally,
the client applications

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:08.050
that we also build.

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:13.920
So we build apps for
mobile, for web, obviously.

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:15.830
And then we're
building smart TV apps.

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:20.190
Samsung is about to be released
in Beta in the next couple

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:24.790
of weeks, and then Apple TV--
the pucks, as I call them--

00:21:24.790 --> 00:21:27.580
Apple TV, Roku, the
sticks, Fire TV--

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:28.080
whatever.

00:21:33.055 --> 00:21:35.180
What we're doing is actually
a lot more complicated

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:38.910
than the original HLS slide
for a bunch of reasons.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:41.120
First of all, we
have permissioning

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:42.542
and licensing to worry about.

00:21:42.542 --> 00:21:44.000
And not only is
it, are you allowed

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:49.290
to access content at all but
is, very specifically, what

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:50.410
content?

00:21:50.410 --> 00:21:53.142
And a lot of this is
based on where you are.

00:21:53.142 --> 00:21:54.600
The two things that
fold into this,

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.267
or the primary thing
that folds into it,

00:21:56.267 --> 00:21:59.400
is sports rights management.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:03.040
Sports are interesting
in that sometimes,

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:04.980
depending on the
sport, you're allowed

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:09.480
to see your home team
when you're away.

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:13.260
So if you're a New Yorker and
you're here in San Francisco--

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:15.720
I think with baseball,
it's called couch rights,

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:17.250
meaning where your couch is.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:18.750
So if your couch
is in New York, you

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:21.760
can see anything your couch can
see, no matter where you are.

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:24.380
I think NFL has something
called hotel rules, where it

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.370
is wherever you physically are.

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:32.340
So I couldn't watch the New
York Jets, but why would I?

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:33.400
NEEVE NIKOO: Hey, hey--

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:33.900
I'm a--

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:34.470
GEIR MAGNUSSON:
You're a Jets fan?

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:35.220
NEEVE NIKOO: Yes.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:36.210
Yes, sadly.

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:37.270
GEIR MAGNUSSON: This is
actually bigger than the talk.

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:38.520
We actually found a Jets fan.

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:39.270
NEEVE NIKOO: Yeah.

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:40.200
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Yeah.

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:42.810
That's-- you know
the joke about--

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:43.888
it's also like the Mets.

00:22:43.888 --> 00:22:45.180
There's a big sign in New York.

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:46.980
It was, welcome
to New York, home

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:50.367
of nine professional sports
teams, and also the Mets.

00:22:50.367 --> 00:22:51.450
NEEVE NIKOO: Thanks, Geir.

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:54.930
GEIR MAGNUSSON: So one of
the things we have to do

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:57.830
is, where you are,
create streams

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:01.035
that switch based on this
rights management stuff.

00:23:01.035 --> 00:23:03.660
And this is where we're going to
go into a little bit of depth.

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:05.460
When we're creating
a video stream,

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:07.380
it's actually a
personalized stream for you.

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:10.020
Every time the player is
asking for that manifest,

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:13.230
we're not just serving it
something out of a CDN.

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:16.140
What we're doing
is a rights check.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.410
We know who you are.

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:20.520
And then we, based on
switching information

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:22.920
that we have on the
incoming streams,

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:24.613
synthesize a channel
for you based

00:23:24.613 --> 00:23:26.280
on whatever your
rights are and whatever

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:27.660
you're supposed to see.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:30.810
So every four seconds, we
do a computation for you.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:32.880
We get what the manifests
are for whatever

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:35.322
streams could be contributing
to what your channel is.

00:23:35.322 --> 00:23:37.530
And then we write out a
manifest specifically for you

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:38.197
and return that.

00:23:46.513 --> 00:23:48.180
So that's really what
we talk about when

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:50.270
we say the real-time
video stream construction.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:52.340
And we'll go into
that in some depth.

00:23:52.340 --> 00:23:54.380
We have to manage DVRs.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:56.310
That's a talk unto itself.

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:59.240
The rights metadata is
what I was referring to.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:01.520
It's how we construct
these streams.

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:04.820
Encoding and storage-- we do
some delivery optimization.

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:06.650
What we can do is, because--

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:08.480
as we're writing
this manifest, we

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:12.140
have the option of choosing
where you get the segment from.

00:24:12.140 --> 00:24:19.040
So if Google CDN is
the most responsive

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:21.260
where you are at
that moment, we might

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.830
write the URLs to pull the
segments out of Google CDN.

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:25.730
But if you were--

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:27.530
maybe Fastly was better for you.

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:28.970
We then use Fastly.

00:24:28.970 --> 00:24:31.370
So this gives us the ability
to optimize the consumer

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:34.170
experience to go for the
lowest latency possible.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:37.250
It also gives us the ability
to easily steer around a CDN

00:24:37.250 --> 00:24:38.330
when there's a failure.

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:42.620
And like everything
else, they fail.

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:45.200
Program, data and disc--
program, comma, data,

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:46.820
and discovery--

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:48.690
we do a lot in search
and personalization.

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:50.732
It's something that we've
been really focused on,

00:24:50.732 --> 00:24:53.900
and it's really the area
of main focus for us

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:56.210
in the consumer
experience going forward.

00:24:56.210 --> 00:24:59.070
We get this metadata,
schedule metadata,

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:02.150
and we have to process it,
normalize it, and then make

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:05.360
it available to the front ends
as well as internal systems

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:07.410
for channel construction.

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:11.920
And then subscriber data
and user management--

00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:14.172
we're a subscription
business direct to consumer.

00:25:14.172 --> 00:25:15.380
We have to take credit cards.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:17.720
We have to do billing.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:20.540
We use Recurly for that.

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:21.740
But that's that section.

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:23.240
NEEVE NIKOO: And
the only thing I'll

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:25.820
add is, when I was talking
about all the different partners

00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:27.320
and components--

00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:30.540
if you look at this slide and
just the number of people,

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:32.480
the number of companies
that are needed,

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.700
this just puts it
in perspective,

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:37.400
on that slide that I showed, how
much actually goes into this.

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:39.150
And we're going to go
into technical depth

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:41.420
on certain components,
but this, I think,

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:43.670
puts into perspective
how tough this is

00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:45.602
and how many moving
parts there are.

00:25:45.602 --> 00:25:48.060
GEIR MAGNUSSON: And it gets
worse when you add advertising.

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:48.230
NEEVE NIKOO: Yeah.

00:25:48.230 --> 00:25:48.860
GEIR MAGNUSSON:
Because at this point,

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:50.777
you're producing video
stream, and then you've

00:25:50.777 --> 00:25:52.310
got to put a layer
of advertising

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:56.470
to personalize each stream.

00:25:56.470 --> 00:25:58.500
So we use GCP stuff.

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:00.610
We're at a Google conference.

00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:04.720
We are primarily Google
Cloud and always have been.

00:26:06.992 --> 00:26:08.200
NEEVE NIKOO: It's the wizard.

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:09.220
It's the magical wizard.

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.650
GEIR MAGNUSSON: I
thought it was the--

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:15.900
I thought they were just
trying to move us along here.

00:26:18.700 --> 00:26:22.830
I'm kind of averse, personally,
to the more advanced

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:24.960
proprietary offerings
that you find in the cloud

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:26.502
provider, honestly.

00:26:26.502 --> 00:26:28.710
So fundamentally, we're
there for the compute network

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:30.870
and storage, which
we find is excellent.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:32.730
It's really good stuff.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:34.320
We do use Kubernetes.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:36.840
I think no talk here would
be complete without somebody

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:38.880
mentioning Kubernetes.

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:39.810
We do use BigQuery.

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:42.120
That is one of those
proprietary technologies

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.580
that we made the
decision to go with.

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:46.110
It's excellent.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:48.600
It is the core engine
behind everything

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:51.400
we do with search
personalization, metrics,

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:53.830
analytics.

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:59.202
We use Cloud Store for
the video segments.

00:26:59.202 --> 00:27:00.660
We have a lot of
stories about that

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:02.610
because it is a general
purpose storage,

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:05.310
and as you can
imagine, we want to be

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.500
able to get the video
segments, as we receive them

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:09.480
from the encoder, to
disk as fast as possible

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:10.630
so we don't lose them.

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:12.172
But at the same
time, they have to be

00:27:12.172 --> 00:27:14.640
available for
reading immediately

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:15.780
because it's a live stream.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:19.970
So we're very sensitive
to the latency there.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:21.990
And then we also use
Data Store, really,

00:27:21.990 --> 00:27:26.440
for manifest and historical
manifest storage.

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:28.510
So I'm not going to
talk about this again.

00:27:28.510 --> 00:27:30.530
I just wanted to,
as a reminder--

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:32.553
how HLS works.

00:27:32.553 --> 00:27:34.720
What we've done is we've
taken that previous diagram

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:36.595
and made it a little
more complicated because

00:27:36.595 --> 00:27:40.210
of this idea that we have
to be synthesizing channels

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:43.460
based on a bunch of factors.

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.203
One factor I didn't mention is--

00:27:45.203 --> 00:27:46.870
especially, it's
important with sports--

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:49.090
the concept of overtime.

00:27:49.090 --> 00:27:52.090
Nobody can predict how
long the game is really

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:54.670
going to go in any sport
that allows overtime.

00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:57.310
So very often, what
we have to do--

00:27:57.310 --> 00:27:59.387
and most people are watching--

00:27:59.387 --> 00:28:00.970
except the things
like BN where you're

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:04.240
getting sports-specific
channels-- a lot of people

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:07.540
consume their sports on your
local affiliate, your local NBC

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:09.620
station here in San Francisco.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:11.290
And they have a
schedule that comprises

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:15.062
local programming, which
might be source number one.

00:28:15.062 --> 00:28:16.770
And then there's the
national feed, which

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:18.310
would be source number two.

00:28:18.310 --> 00:28:21.340
And what we have
to do is we have

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:24.310
what's called SCTE signaling
information that tell us

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:25.850
when to switch between streams.

00:28:25.850 --> 00:28:27.730
So you may have
local programming

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:29.830
up to the start of
a baseball game,

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:31.750
and then you switch
to the national feed

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:32.792
to get the baseball game.

00:28:32.792 --> 00:28:34.375
And then you might
have to switch back

00:28:34.375 --> 00:28:36.880
once the game is theoretically
over because then there's

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:39.310
"Judge Judy" or
whatever is shown.

00:28:39.310 --> 00:28:41.380
But when you have
overtime or extended play,

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:44.740
like in extra innings,
this becomes a problem

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:47.380
because you do not want to
switch back to "Judge Judy"

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:49.360
when the game is
going into overtime,

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:52.270
because that's usually the
most exciting part of a game.

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:54.940
And it produces
tremendous amounts of ire

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:56.530
from your customers--

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:57.850
true hate.

00:28:57.850 --> 00:29:00.430
Nothing is worse than,
say, dropping off

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.770
the third overtime
in a hockey game

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:04.780
and switching back to
the original programming.

00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:07.000
They will come after you.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:08.530
So we have these
multiple encoding

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:11.050
streams that we're storing.

00:29:11.050 --> 00:29:13.390
And what's happening is this
playlist decisioning system

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:16.810
is using some of this metadata
to decide which source to give

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:18.615
you when you ask for a channel.

00:29:18.615 --> 00:29:19.990
So in terms of a
flow, the player

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:24.490
is saying to an authorization
service, hey, can I watch this?

00:29:24.490 --> 00:29:25.690
And here's my credentials.

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:29.590
And the auth service gives
it a token back, says, sure.

00:29:29.590 --> 00:29:32.460
Here, give this to
the playlist service.

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:36.290
The player will then ask the
playlist decisioning system,

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:39.850
give me my manifest
for this channel.

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:41.920
The player decisioning
system will,

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:43.990
based on where you
are in time and what's

00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:46.750
supposed to be happening,
choose the source stream

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:49.900
and provide a manifest
to you that tells you

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:51.370
what the segments are next.

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:54.160
It can also rewrite
the URLs to get them.

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:56.380
So we can choose
among a set of CDNs

00:29:56.380 --> 00:29:59.030
that we work with, which CDN
is going to deliver to you.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:01.510
And then the player says, all
right, give me the segment,

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:02.300
to the right CDN.

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.270
The CDN then gets
it from origin.

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:06.910
It's not that
interesting, that part.

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:09.130
And then lather,
rinse, and repeat--

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:12.360
you ask for your next manifest.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:15.730
The playlist decisioning system
can choose a different stream

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:19.050
based on whatever really
you're supposed to see,

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:22.030
and then you may choose even a
different CDN because something

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:24.250
happened with the first
CDN that you were using.

00:30:24.250 --> 00:30:27.802
So this is really how
we produce streams.

00:30:27.802 --> 00:30:29.260
And if you think
about it, it means

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:34.800
that every single stream
that we produce is unique.

00:30:34.800 --> 00:30:37.560
They're going to look the same,
but they are, in theory, unique

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:39.310
because we are computing
them specifically

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:41.050
for a given player.

00:30:45.225 --> 00:30:47.350
Now, this is a business
where we take all the money

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:48.970
that the consumers
give us, and then

00:30:48.970 --> 00:30:50.470
we take some other
money that we get

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:52.387
from either advertising
or VCs, and we give it

00:30:52.387 --> 00:30:55.420
all to the content owners.

00:30:55.420 --> 00:30:58.700
This is not in itself a
sustainable business model.

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:00.800
It's a very difficult business.

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:03.790
So where the
financials come in is--

00:31:03.790 --> 00:31:06.850
really, advertising is the core
monetization for the company.

00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:09.710
And it, too, is an
interesting technical problem.

00:31:09.710 --> 00:31:12.190
I used to be the
CTO of AppNexus.

00:31:12.190 --> 00:31:13.930
When I left AppNexus,
I thought, OK, I've

00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:15.222
done on my time in advertising.

00:31:15.222 --> 00:31:17.020
I will never go back to this.

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:19.630
And I find now that,
really, most of my attention

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:22.277
is being spent on advertising.

00:31:22.277 --> 00:31:24.610
NEEVE NIKOO: And Google knows
nothing about advertising.

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:25.060
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Sorry?

00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:26.380
NEEVE NIKOO: We don't know
anything about advertising.

00:31:26.380 --> 00:31:27.375
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Right.

00:31:27.375 --> 00:31:29.500
Yeah, don't ask about that
division of the company.

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:30.792
We're here to talk about Cloud.

00:31:30.792 --> 00:31:32.980
NEEVE NIKOO: Right, right.

00:31:32.980 --> 00:31:34.990
GEIR MAGNUSSON: As we
said, every video stream

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:36.930
is unique and independent.

00:31:36.930 --> 00:31:39.520
And that gives us the
ability to produce

00:31:39.520 --> 00:31:43.400
advertising that is unique
and independent for each user.

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:47.000
This is the holy
grail for advertisers,

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:50.080
is to be able to target
you specifically.

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:53.380
And it's going to be
watched, unlike like, say,

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:59.450
web display, where most of
it is below the fold anyway.

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:01.630
This is something--
your TV is up there.

00:32:01.630 --> 00:32:05.215
And this is not a distracting
thing in the corner.

00:32:05.215 --> 00:32:06.340
I'm not going to apologize.

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:07.390
That's how television works.

00:32:07.390 --> 00:32:09.310
But at some point, you
go to commercial break

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:11.320
and the commercials are shown.

00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:12.890
So these are ads that are shown.

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:15.100
There are humans
that watch them.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:19.260
So we can determine
in real time.

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:21.960
And this is not a new
concept for anybody

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:24.240
that has been doing
advertising the last 10 years.

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:26.820
But for television,
it's kind of a new idea.

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:28.320
And honestly, the
television buyers,

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:30.600
the people who are buying
advertising for television,

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:32.383
don't get it yet.

00:32:32.383 --> 00:32:34.050
They don't really
understand that we can

00:32:34.050 --> 00:32:37.900
target to the individual user.

00:32:37.900 --> 00:32:40.080
So how does it
work, technically?

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:41.550
So if you remember
what we talked

00:32:41.550 --> 00:32:44.490
about before-- the player
talks to the playlist service,

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:47.850
which gets manifests
and the cycle continues.

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:50.913
What we do is we put an
ad stitcher in front.

00:32:50.913 --> 00:32:52.830
And what an ad stitcher
looks like-- it's just

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:54.553
a proxy in some ways.

00:32:54.553 --> 00:32:56.220
And when the player
asks for a manifest,

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:58.095
the ad stitcher goes to
the playlist service,

00:32:58.095 --> 00:33:01.110
and the manifest is
produced, and it's returned.

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:02.265
Now, as it's coming back--

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:07.780
to get an advertisement,
there are queue points.

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:10.680
There might be a little line
in the manifest that says,

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:12.280
queue out here.

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:13.790
And you can do it
for 60 seconds.

00:33:13.790 --> 00:33:15.460
That's a 60 second ad break.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:18.040
Now, there still is going
to be video segments listed.

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:20.740
Those are the video segments
on the original stream.

00:33:20.740 --> 00:33:22.870
So that would actually
be the advertisement

00:33:22.870 --> 00:33:26.598
that the content owners, say,
CNN, has on their channel.

00:33:26.598 --> 00:33:29.140
But as part of the agreement we
have with the content owners,

00:33:29.140 --> 00:33:32.110
they give us some amount of
time per hour, say, one to two

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:33.680
minutes, two to three minutes.

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:36.280
So when we get our ad
break, what will happen then

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:38.960
is MediaTailor will
look at the manifest,

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:40.930
the ad stitcher,
looks at the manifest,

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:45.610
sees that an ad break is coming,
and then it makes an ad call.

00:33:45.610 --> 00:33:50.020
And it calls-- we have
a proxy because we

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:57.050
wanted to be able to have some
control over in real time.

00:33:57.050 --> 00:33:58.580
We were calling
which ad server we

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:00.788
want to work with and logging
and all sorts of stuff.

00:34:00.788 --> 00:34:02.590
It's obviously a
good idea to do.

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:05.990
And what we do is we choose
among a set of ad servers or ad

00:34:05.990 --> 00:34:06.950
partners.

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:08.480
FreeWheel, you've
probably heard of.

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:10.429
It is owned by Comcast.

00:34:10.429 --> 00:34:13.250
It's a very popular,
very big video ad server.

00:34:13.250 --> 00:34:17.690
SpringServe is a lesser known
but an excellent ad server.

00:34:17.690 --> 00:34:20.929
APS is Amazon's
publishing system.

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:22.460
They do buying.

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:25.500
And then the ad servers
talk to, basically,

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:28.550
SSPs or other demand partners
who figure out who has an ad

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:31.520
and wants that specific
person or that specific device

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:33.179
or whatever.

00:34:33.179 --> 00:34:37.219
Then the ad slot is filled
up with ads, basically,

00:34:37.219 --> 00:34:38.360
links to ads.

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:41.090
And that whole pile is then
sent back up to MediaTailor,

00:34:41.090 --> 00:34:42.620
to the ad stitcher.

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:44.449
Now, these are MP4s.

00:34:44.449 --> 00:34:47.300
They can't actually be
inserted in an HLS stream

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:49.880
because HLS has multiple
variants at different bit

00:34:49.880 --> 00:34:51.100
rates.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:52.670
What the ad stitcher
has to do is

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:55.880
take the advertisements
that came back from the ad

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:57.350
decisioning side of things.

00:34:57.350 --> 00:34:59.360
It has to transcode them.

00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:01.700
It has to turn them into
the same variant rates

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:03.560
that we have the
main streams on,

00:35:03.560 --> 00:35:05.060
which means that,
for the first time

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:06.620
it ever sees an
advertisement, it

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:09.290
can't show it because
it hasn't transcoded it.

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:11.330
You can't transcode
them that fast.

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:14.210
So it transcodes them,
puts it into a CDN,

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:16.310
and remembers that it
has transcoded that ad

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:19.550
so the next time that ad comes
through, which it will, the ad

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:20.690
is ready to play.

00:35:20.690 --> 00:35:23.690
And it rewrites the
manifest so that instead

00:35:23.690 --> 00:35:25.850
of getting the video
chunks that are

00:35:25.850 --> 00:35:28.860
from the underlying
streams, CNN,

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:30.560
it actually then just
gives video chunks

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:32.260
that are advertising chunks.

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:33.920
So it's a seamless process.

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:37.280
The player generally doesn't
know what's going on.

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:40.160
There's some variance to that.

00:35:40.160 --> 00:35:42.360
Sometimes the player can
do beaconing as well.

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:44.450
But fundamentally, from
a playback perspective,

00:35:44.450 --> 00:35:45.740
the player has no idea.

00:35:45.740 --> 00:35:48.350
It's just getting video segment,
video segment, video segment.

00:35:48.350 --> 00:35:51.140
Video segments happen to switch
from original content video

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:53.090
segment to advertising segment.

00:35:53.090 --> 00:35:54.890
And then when
there's a queue in,

00:35:54.890 --> 00:35:58.523
basically, it's where the
playlist service is saying,

00:35:58.523 --> 00:35:59.690
all right, ad break is over.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:01.280
You've got to go
back to content.

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:03.200
Then MediaTailor
stops doing anything

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:05.570
and just keeps them
flowing through.

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:08.990
And that's really how
server-side ad stitching works.

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:13.130
Fairly simple in
theory, but a lot

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:17.120
of moving parts, a lot of
things that can go wrong.

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:22.660
If any of you have worked
with advertising technologies,

00:36:22.660 --> 00:36:23.930
they can be a mess.

00:36:23.930 --> 00:36:25.560
And these are.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:30.500
So I think that was it in
terms of the overall tech

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:31.160
part of things.

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:33.410
NEEVE NIKOO: Yeah, and I
guess the only thing I'll say

00:36:33.410 --> 00:36:36.740
is all of that that
he just explained

00:36:36.740 --> 00:36:41.690
happens in between when you--
literally, the game is on

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:43.460
and then goes to halftime.

00:36:43.460 --> 00:36:46.250
That little blip,
all of that literally

00:36:46.250 --> 00:36:49.970
happens in the backend just
to serve you the correct ad.

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:53.060
So it's a very,
very quick process

00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:55.820
that needs to happen in
order to stitch all of that

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:57.900
together to serve you
that advertisement.

00:36:57.900 --> 00:36:59.990
So I hope-- I don't
know if it's just me.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:02.090
It's a really,
really hard problem

00:37:02.090 --> 00:37:04.130
that happens super, super fast.

00:37:04.130 --> 00:37:05.720
He explained that
over 5, 10 minutes,

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:07.460
and that happens in
[SNAPS] Like that.

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:09.980
So it's just to put
it in perspective,

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:12.080
all of the moving
pieces to do that.

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:14.420
Sorry.

00:37:14.420 --> 00:37:16.680
GEIR MAGNUSSON: So in
terms of best practices,

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:18.722
we've been on this journey
for a couple of years.

00:37:21.588 --> 00:37:23.130
We didn't do what
people normally do,

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:25.950
which is do on prem so they
can control everything.

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:28.460
As you imagine, we're
really sensitive to network.

00:37:28.460 --> 00:37:30.200
We're really
sensitive to compute.

00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:32.768
We're really sensitive
to storage latency.

00:37:32.768 --> 00:37:34.310
But we did it all
on the Cloud, and I

00:37:34.310 --> 00:37:36.860
guess we have some best
practices that we've learned.

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:40.850
And I'll say together,
because honestly, Google--

00:37:40.850 --> 00:37:43.485
when you're working with a
cloud provider like this,

00:37:43.485 --> 00:37:45.860
they are your operations team,
or part of your operations

00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:49.320
team, whether you
realize it or not.

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:54.350
And our relationship is to
the point where it really

00:37:54.350 --> 00:37:56.390
is a partnership.

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:59.480
That's really the
last point here.

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:01.497
They're not just a vendor.

00:38:01.497 --> 00:38:03.080
You shouldn't treat
them like a vendor

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:05.253
if it's something
core to your business.

00:38:05.253 --> 00:38:07.420
And they shouldn't treat
you like a customer or just

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:08.480
a customer.

00:38:08.480 --> 00:38:12.330
You need to be part
of the same team.

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:15.740
We have weekly calls
that they participate

00:38:15.740 --> 00:38:17.750
with us and with
other third parties

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:21.348
that we work with because
we're so sensitive to things

00:38:21.348 --> 00:38:21.890
like network.

00:38:21.890 --> 00:38:23.432
Their networking
team is on the call.

00:38:23.432 --> 00:38:27.680
It is not just the usual
front-facing customer

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:28.470
engineers.

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:31.520
We can get deep into
their teams because they

00:38:31.520 --> 00:38:35.610
have a vested interest
in us succeeding.

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:37.830
Other ideas is that, look,
these cloud systems are

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:39.210
built for general purpose use.

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:41.130
They're not built for us.

00:38:41.130 --> 00:38:44.190
They're built for
thousands of customers,

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:47.560
multi-tenancy, a wide
range of workloads.

00:38:47.560 --> 00:38:49.230
So test your use case.

00:38:49.230 --> 00:38:50.550
It's kind of obvious.

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:52.570
We're very sensitive to latency.

00:38:52.570 --> 00:38:54.270
And we have to
design around some

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:56.010
of the uncertainty in
latency that we see

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:58.770
in these cloud-based systems.

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:04.973
Cloud systems behaviors are
subject to change, sometimes

00:39:04.973 --> 00:39:07.140
just due to failure, but
that's not that interesting

00:39:07.140 --> 00:39:09.480
because everything fails.

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:12.000
Sometimes it feels like
the systems actually

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:20.390
change their behavioral
properties based on load.

00:39:20.390 --> 00:39:22.910
Normally, we have no problem
with hotkeys in a data store,

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:25.610
and all of a sudden keys become
hotkeys and latency increases

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:26.770
or fail.

00:39:26.770 --> 00:39:29.520
These are things you just have
to be aware of will happen,

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:32.540
and it's due to other
activities within the Cloud

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:36.170
because these are
shared systems.

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:39.500
And then this idea for
proprietary technology--

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:42.447
choose carefully, because what
is a quick win today-- like,

00:39:42.447 --> 00:39:44.280
oh, let me just use
that because it's quick,

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:45.620
and I can get the system up--

00:39:45.620 --> 00:39:47.620
is next year's tech debt.

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:50.870
And it's sometimes awfully hard
to back out of these things.

00:39:50.870 --> 00:39:54.560
We made a decision to go with
BigQuery, very happy with it.

00:39:54.560 --> 00:39:56.870
It is a proprietary
lock-in kind of thing.

00:39:56.870 --> 00:39:58.730
We are kind of stuck.

00:39:58.730 --> 00:39:59.480
We're not unhappy.

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:00.500
We don't want to go.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:02.000
But if you think
about what it would

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.920
take to move all of
this data out of Google

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:06.210
into somewhere else,
it's not a problem

00:40:06.210 --> 00:40:08.337
I even want to think about.

00:40:08.337 --> 00:40:10.670
I think that-- do you have
anything you want to add on--

00:40:10.670 --> 00:40:16.610
NEEVE NIKOO: I think, just
to bring the point home--

00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:18.320
we have these weekly calls.

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:19.673
What is that weekly call?

00:40:19.673 --> 00:40:21.590
There's going to be many
different people that

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:23.030
are all always involved.

00:40:23.030 --> 00:40:25.970
All those partners that you
saw will have multiple calls

00:40:25.970 --> 00:40:27.410
with different partners.

00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:30.980
And it's just really being aware
of the different services that

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:33.800
are segmented and who's
all involved and where.

00:40:33.800 --> 00:40:38.240
An I think just being an
honest, open, transparent--

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:42.680
not to be cliché, but DevOpsian
culture and environment really

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:44.840
helped, I think, make
us successful together,

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:47.660
because we understand that
there's all these moving pieces

00:40:47.660 --> 00:40:51.110
and all these different parts
and partners that are involved.

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:54.050
And it's just about,
together, solving this problem

00:40:54.050 --> 00:40:57.110
and understanding
where each piece fits.

00:40:57.110 --> 00:40:59.468
And I think it really,
really helped us really

00:40:59.468 --> 00:41:00.260
both be successful.

00:41:00.260 --> 00:41:01.135
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Yeah.

00:41:01.135 --> 00:41:03.200
NEEVE NIKOO: And
move really fast.

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:04.520
GEIR MAGNUSSON: I think there
are Google engineers that

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:06.978
spend more time talking with
some of our technical partners

00:41:06.978 --> 00:41:07.570
than I do.

00:41:07.570 --> 00:41:08.320
NEEVE NIKOO: Yeah.

00:41:08.320 --> 00:41:09.403
GEIR MAGNUSSON: It's true.

00:41:09.403 --> 00:41:11.840
And I think it's great
because they are world class

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:13.950
experts in the things
they're working on,

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:15.950
and they can really get
to the root of a problem

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:17.152
when there is one.

00:41:17.152 --> 00:41:18.110
And there are problems.

00:41:18.110 --> 00:41:18.890
NEEVE NIKOO: Yeah.

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:20.432
GEIR MAGNUSSON:
There always will be.

00:41:22.030 --> 00:41:24.660
Our future, we're trying
to bring key core services

00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:25.160
in-house.

00:41:25.160 --> 00:41:29.548
We'll continue to build more new
things on the Google platform.

00:41:29.548 --> 00:41:32.090
Multi-cloud-- it's amazing that
the marketing people let this

00:41:32.090 --> 00:41:33.240
through.

00:41:33.240 --> 00:41:35.330
But it's one of the
things we're doing,

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:37.580
mainly driven by
external forces.

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:41.210
There are content streams that
are coming into other cloud

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:43.070
environments, not to Google.

00:41:43.070 --> 00:41:44.750
And we have to go
over there to be

00:41:44.750 --> 00:41:46.760
able to receive them
and encode them,

00:41:46.760 --> 00:41:49.460
and then we can
