00:42:32.360 --> 00:42:34.520
AUDIENCE: I got one
question on DVR.

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:40.370
How are you dealing with
massive parallel DVR recording?

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:42.400
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Lots of storage.

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:48.530
It's a big expense,
but we have the--

00:42:48.530 --> 00:42:51.340
think about what a DVR
recording is in the cloud.

00:42:51.340 --> 00:42:53.030
We have all the segments.

00:42:53.030 --> 00:42:55.910
They land because where we're
taking them off the encoders

00:42:55.910 --> 00:42:57.080
and putting them in.

00:42:57.080 --> 00:42:59.675
The DVR process happens in the
background and makes copies.

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:07.380
That's really how it works.

00:43:07.380 --> 00:43:09.230
And it's a large
storage cost for us.

00:43:09.230 --> 00:43:13.250
We use different tiers of
storage for this purpose,

00:43:13.250 --> 00:43:19.800
because unlike the first line
of storage for the CDN origins,

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:21.750
which have to be fast--

00:43:21.750 --> 00:43:24.260
wish they were faster--

00:43:24.260 --> 00:43:26.060
DVR doesn't have to be as fast.

00:43:26.060 --> 00:43:27.650
You can accept some latency.

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:30.470
When somebody hits
play on a DVR,

00:43:30.470 --> 00:43:33.260
it can be pulled out
of the nearline storage

00:43:33.260 --> 00:43:34.010
and start playing.

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:39.470
AUDIENCE: Google has
