00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:42.710
Why not use them instead?

00:43:44.963 --> 00:43:46.880
GEIR MAGNUSSON: They
have a suite of services.

00:43:46.880 --> 00:43:48.760
They have an encoding service.

00:43:48.760 --> 00:43:51.688
I think have an ad stitcher.

00:43:51.688 --> 00:43:52.730
They don't do what we do.

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:54.313
They don't have the
rights management.

00:43:54.313 --> 00:43:56.180
They don't handle
the things that we

00:43:56.180 --> 00:44:01.620
needed to do to be able
to license this content

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:05.300
and distribute it.

00:44:05.300 --> 00:44:07.980
These kind of
services, like Anvato,

00:44:07.980 --> 00:44:10.130
they were pointed out
on the original slide

00:44:10.130 --> 00:44:11.180
by content owners.

00:44:11.180 --> 00:44:14.900
If I'm NBC, I know exactly
what I'm going to do

00:44:14.900 --> 00:44:19.852
and don't have to worry about
licensing in the same way we

00:44:19.852 --> 00:44:22.310
do because they are the ones
that are putting the licensing

00:44:22.310 --> 00:44:23.640
constraints on us.

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:27.940
So I think it's a good
set of building blocks.

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:30.010
We had to do something
different, fundamentally,

00:44:30.010 --> 00:44:31.930
with playlist generation.

00:44:31.930 --> 00:44:35.260
And then honestly,
given how sensitive

00:44:35.260 --> 00:44:37.920
we are to a good
video experience,

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:39.610
we are trying to do
more and more things

00:44:39.610 --> 00:44:41.260
like encoding in-house.

00:44:41.260 --> 00:44:43.882
So at least if something
goes wrong, it's our fault,

00:44:43.882 --> 00:44:45.340
and it's something
that we can fix.

00:44:45.340 --> 00:44:47.170
If it's a third-party
provider, there's

00:44:47.170 --> 00:44:51.140
not a lot you can do sometimes.

00:44:51.140 --> 00:44:54.230
AUDIENCE: Just to clarify a
couple of icons on the slide,

00:44:54.230 --> 00:44:58.560
I'm guessing that you use GCS
for video segment data storage.

00:44:58.560 --> 00:45:01.160
But I was curious what you use
for the video stream metadata

00:45:01.160 --> 00:45:03.287
storage, if that's GCS
or if it's faster or--

00:45:03.287 --> 00:45:04.370
GEIR MAGNUSSON: You mean--

00:45:04.370 --> 00:45:05.270
AUDIENCE: --different
in any way?

00:45:05.270 --> 00:45:06.310
GEIR MAGNUSSON: The manifests?

00:45:06.310 --> 00:45:07.010
AUDIENCE: Yeah, the manifests.

00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:07.510
Yeah.

00:45:07.510 --> 00:45:10.320
GEIR MAGNUSSON:
So the manifests,

00:45:10.320 --> 00:45:13.490
they go in a data store
right now because we

00:45:13.490 --> 00:45:17.930
need them time-ordered.

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:20.690
We are switching to a
different system which, again,

00:45:20.690 --> 00:45:22.738
preserves that time order.

00:45:22.738 --> 00:45:25.280
We think it's going to be a--
basically, if you think about--

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:30.800
Kafka is a time-ordered
list of stuff

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:34.958
that is permanent until
you roll it off the back.

00:45:34.958 --> 00:45:37.250
That solves that problem as
well, so we do it that way.

00:45:37.250 --> 00:45:39.138
AUDIENCE: So switching
to Kafka, then?

00:45:39.138 --> 00:45:41.180
GEIR MAGNUSSON: We are
testing a solution that is

00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:44.300
based on a large Kafka cluster.

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:46.930
It's a flat load input
because you've got

00:45:46.930 --> 00:45:48.920
800 channels or 1,000 channels.

00:45:48.920 --> 00:45:51.960
They have a completely
predictable and stable load.

00:45:51.960 --> 00:45:54.530
So that's actually easy
to ingest in store,

00:45:54.530 --> 00:45:57.750
and the variable load
is on the output.

00:45:57.750 --> 00:46:00.678
So how many people, concurrent
streams, are coming in?

00:46:00.678 --> 00:46:02.720
And it's an easy problem
for something like Kafka

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:05.303
because you confront it with a
cache in front that keeps, say,

00:46:05.303 --> 00:46:07.640
the last 15 minutes of
manifests in memory.

00:46:07.640 --> 00:46:10.130
And if everybody's watching
the exact same program,

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:10.950
it's not a problem.

00:46:10.950 --> 00:46:12.590
You're just pulling it around.

00:46:12.590 --> 00:46:13.298
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

