GEIR MAGNUSSON: What
happens is the ad stitcher

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:45.390
makes the ad call to us.

00:46:45.390 --> 00:46:49.050
It goes to an ad server, just
a conventional video ad server.

00:46:49.050 --> 00:46:51.510
And that makes the call
to something like DFP.

00:46:51.510 --> 00:46:54.900
So that might be
somebody is buying Roku.

00:46:54.900 --> 00:46:56.670
They really want to
buy Roku impressions.

00:46:56.670 --> 00:47:00.460
They give us tags that
come from DFP's ad server.

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:02.742
Our ad server will call
that just to see, hey,

00:47:02.742 --> 00:47:03.450
do you want this?

00:47:03.450 --> 00:47:05.305
And it'll return
either a VAST or not.

00:47:05.305 --> 00:47:07.180
That all gets packaged
together and returned.

00:47:07.180 --> 00:47:09.810
So it's not like there's a
connection between the ad

00:47:09.810 --> 00:47:12.630
stitcher and the demand
partners directly.

00:47:12.630 --> 00:47:15.185
The ad server is making the
calls to the demand partners

00:47:15.185 --> 00:47:15.810
and going back.

00:47:18.320 --> 00:47:19.760
We don't use DFP
as our ad server.

00:47:23.063 --> 00:47:23.730
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:47:23.730 --> 00:47:25.800
Are you currently
using or have plans

00:47:25.800 --> 00:47:29.310
to use any machine learning
services for content

00:47:29.310 --> 00:47:31.950
delivery or ad delivery?

00:47:31.950 --> 00:47:35.670
GEIR MAGNUSSON: We're
experimenting on both of those.

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:42.190
It's primarily in the area of
recommendation and similar.

00:47:42.190 --> 00:47:43.800
But there are some
interesting uses

00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:49.682
for detection of advertisement,
detection of sentiment,

00:47:49.682 --> 00:47:51.390
to understand what's
happening in a game.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:53.280
If we could
automatically know a game

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:56.610
is going into overtime by
understanding what the game

00:47:56.610 --> 00:47:59.017
action is, or the clock,
or the scoreboard,

00:47:59.017 --> 00:48:00.600
it would make our
lives a lot simpler,

00:48:00.600 --> 00:48:02.550
although it's a fairly
sophisticated way

00:48:02.550 --> 00:48:04.758
to do it versus just having
somebody watch it and hit

00:48:04.758 --> 00:48:05.413
a button.

00:48:05.413 --> 00:48:06.246
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:48:12.160 --> 00:48:14.070
AUDIENCE: Hey.

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:17.173
If you have a critical
failure in the ad pipeline,

00:48:17.173 --> 00:48:19.340
what does the experience
look like for the customer?

00:48:19.340 --> 00:48:22.190
Do you try and fail
in favor of them or--

00:48:22.190 --> 00:48:23.910
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Sorry,
in which pipeline?

00:48:23.910 --> 00:48:25.660
AUDIENCE: So this whole
ad pipeline, here,

00:48:25.660 --> 00:48:28.372
the thing that delivers
the adverts to the customer

00:48:28.372 --> 00:48:29.330
in some kind of break--

00:48:29.330 --> 00:48:29.690
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Yep.

00:48:29.690 --> 00:48:31.430
AUDIENCE: --if they saw some
kind of critical failure,

00:48:31.430 --> 00:48:34.070
and you can't deliver those ads,
what does the experience look

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:34.810
like for the customer?

00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:35.602
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Ah.

00:48:35.602 --> 00:48:38.220
This is actually the best
failure mode we have.

00:48:38.220 --> 00:48:40.010
Because, if you remember--

00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:42.500
say you're watching CNN.

00:48:42.500 --> 00:48:44.220
CNN goes into an ad break.

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:45.405
There are CNN ads there.

00:48:45.405 --> 00:48:46.780
CNN is putting
advertising there.

00:48:46.780 --> 00:48:49.140
It's the same stream
you get on TV.

00:48:49.140 --> 00:48:51.920
So if there's a failure
to deliver, to get

00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:55.130
an advertisement, or to
transcode the advertisement,

00:48:55.130 --> 00:48:57.110
then just the underlying
stream just plays

00:48:57.110 --> 00:48:59.180
and the user has no
idea something happened.

00:48:59.180 --> 00:49:00.805
That's assuming the
ad stitcher doesn't

00:49:00.805 --> 00:49:02.178
do something really stupid.

00:49:02.178 --> 00:49:03.220
But it generally doesn't.

00:49:12.095 --> 00:49:13.530
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:49:13.530 --> 00:49:17.460
Is your ad stitcher having
to decode and reencode

00:49:17.460 --> 00:49:19.315
so that it can--

00:49:19.315 --> 00:49:20.190
GEIR MAGNUSSON: Yeah.

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:20.690
Yeah.

00:49:20.690 --> 00:49:23.190
So when an advertisement
is first seen--

00:49:23.190 --> 00:49:26.880
so suppose it's a 30 second
ad for Ford something.

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:28.470
I don't know.

00:49:28.470 --> 00:49:31.260
It has a unique identifier,
usually just the URL for it,

00:49:31.260 --> 00:49:33.540
for the MP4.

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:35.730
MediaTailor keeps a list
of all the advertisements

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:37.230
that it knows about
and has encoded.

00:49:37.230 --> 00:49:40.980
And if it's not on that list,
it will run a transcode process

00:49:40.980 --> 00:49:44.020
in the same way we transcode
the incoming stream,

00:49:44.020 --> 00:49:45.600
turn it into the
bit rate variance

00:49:45.600 --> 00:49:50.550
that we're using
so that it can be

00:49:50.550 --> 00:49:54.030
slotted right into whatever
bit rate stream the player

00:49:54.030 --> 00:49:55.140
is playing.

00:49:55.140 --> 00:49:58.140
That means, of course, that
the first time an ad is seen

00:49:58.140 --> 00:50:00.780
by the ad stitcher, it
cannot be played to the user.

00:50:00.780 --> 00:50:02.940
And that's a case where
there is that failure mode.

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:07.632
We just don't show an ad and the
underlying stream is playing.

00:50:07.632 --> 00:50:08.590
I should have drawn it.

00:50:08.590 --> 00:50:09.280
I'm sorry.

00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:10.200
There should've been
a little loop here

00:50:10.200 --> 00:50:12.100
saying this is where
the encoding happens.

00:50:12.100 --> 00:50:13.790
But then once it
has it, it's cached.

00:50:13.790 --> 00:50:16.020
It can serve that ad
next time the decisioning

00:50:16.020 --> 00:50:17.700
system says, show that ad.

00:50:17.700 --> 00:50:20.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

